] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6e785fe-8b18-56e6-9291-7b0b3e83aeef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452141} :answer-field/id #uuid "4105ae7e-75bf-54ba-80a7-8977f2a90571" :answer-field/key "$\\{0,{t}^{3}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}_{3}[t]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bf9e33e0-00b4-5d7d-a1a7-55573563c2a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-94850" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${R}_{3}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $3.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$\\{0,{t}^{3}\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${R}_{3}[t]$” is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n1\\cdot 0+0\\cdot {t}^{3}=0,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the first coefficient is nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent. In general, any set that contains a zero-vector is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement “$-{t}^{3}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{0,{t}^{3}\\}$” is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n-{t}^{3}=0\\cdot 0+(-1)\\cdot {t}^{3}.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$\\{0,{t}^{3}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}_{3}[t]$” is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$-{t}^{3}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{0,{t}^{3}\\}$” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360519 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c0f5ce7-fdc1-5450-becc-3455d6e589d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452143} {:answer/id #uuid "9eaa2dd6-e820-58e5-8ff1-8304461c433e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answe